{
  "id": "py-23-strings-never-change",
  "track": "python",
  "unit": "py-u4",
  "order": 23,
  "title": "Strings never change",
  "summary": "String methods like upper, strip and replace make a new string. Keep the result by storing it.",
  "language": "python",
  "minutes": 25,
  "visual": "memory",
  "visuals": [
    "refs",
    "memory"
  ],
  "intro": {
    "text": "A string can **never be changed** once it is made. Trying to swap one letter stops the program:\n\n```\nword = \"jam\"\nword[0] = \"h\"\n```\n\ngives **TypeError: 'str' object does not support item assignment**.\n\nInstead, strings have **methods**: tools you call with a dot after the string and brackets at the end, like `word.upper()`. Each one builds a **new** string and gives it back:\n\n- `upper()` and `lower()`: all capitals or all small letters\n- `title()`: a capital at the start of each word\n- `strip()`: removes spaces at both ends (not in the middle)\n- `replace(old, new)`: swaps every `old` for `new`\n\nThe old string is left exactly as it was. To keep the new one, store it: `word = word.upper()`. That line does not change the old string; it moves the name `word` to the new one. The **refs** panel draws each name as an arrow to the object it points at.\n\nMethods can be **chained**: `text.strip().lower()` runs left to right, each method working on the string the one before made.",
    "analogy": "A printed label: you cannot rub out a letter on it. To get different text you print a new label and move your name tag onto it. The old label still says what it always said."
  },
